#
# Every five minutes, follow.sh compares the kernel the next boot will use with the stamp beside each
# registered module. When they agree, which is almost always, it exits; when they differ, a new kernel
# is on disk and not booted yet, and that is the window to rebuild. The upgrade and stop hooks cover
# the same ground; this is the layer that depends on nothing but cron. With nothing registered it is
# one directory listing.
#
# TICK=1 says that this is the tick (2026-10-29): after a failed fetch of a kernel's sources, it waits
# an hour before fetching the same ones again, where the hooks and Follow now fetch at once.

*/5 * * * * root /usr/bin/env TICK=1 /bin/sh /usr/local/opnsense/scripts/kernelfollow/follow.sh > /dev/null 2>&1
